		DVB-S2X VL-SNR Network Streaming Layer
		======================================

This is the optional network streaming module for the DVB-S2X VL-SNR
modem library.  It provides platform abstraction and lock-free ring
buffers for TCP/UDP transport of IQ samples and decoded streams.

The module is designed for satellite communication systems requiring
real-time performance and deterministic latency.


Building
--------

	make			Build libdvbs2x_streaming.a
	make install		Install the library and headers
	make clean		Clean build artifacts


Components
----------

Platform Abstraction Layer (dvbs2x_platform.h)

  Cross-platform socket operations (Linux/Windows), thread management
  (POSIX/Win32), real-time scheduling (SCHED_FIFO, CPU affinity),
  nanosecond-precision timing, and memory locking for real-time systems.

  All platform selection is compile-time via preprocessor macros.
  Zero runtime overhead: inline functions, no virtual dispatch.

Lock-Free Ring Buffer (dvbs2x_ringbuf.h)

  Single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) lock-free ring buffer following
  Linux kernel kfifo design.  Power-of-2 capacity for efficient modulo
  via bitwise AND.  Always-incrementing indices (wrap at 2^32).

  Memory barriers ensure correct ordering without locks.  Cache-line
  alignment prevents false sharing between producer and consumer.
  Zero-copy peek operations for packet inspection.  Batch operations
  amortize atomic overhead.

Protocol Layer (dvbs2x_protocol.h)

  Frame format for network streaming with 16-byte header, CRC-32
  integrity checking (IEEE 802.3), and support for fragmentation.

  Frame format:
    [0-3]   u32 magic        0x53325856 ("XVS2" big-endian)
    [4-7]   u32 sequence     Sequence number
    [8-9]   u16 payload_len  Payload bytes (0-65520)
    [10]    u8  frame_type   IQ_SAMPLES/TS_PACKETS/BBFRAME
    [11]    u8  flags        MORE_FRAGS + frag_offset[21:16]
    [12-13] u16 frag_offset  Fragment offset (8-byte units)
    [14-15] u16 stream_id    Stream multiplex ID
    [...]   payload
    [...]   u32 crc32        CRC-32 over header[4-15] + payload

  All multi-byte fields in network byte order (big-endian).
  Maximum payload: 65520 bytes per frame.

TCP Transport (dvbs2x_tcp.h)

  Connection-oriented reliable transport for IQ samples and decoded
  streams.  Provides server (listen/accept) and client (connect) modes
  with timeout support and statistics tracking.

  Features:
    - Automatic retry on EINTR (signal interruption)
    - Send/recv loop until all bytes transferred (critical!)
    - TCP_NODELAY for low latency (disables Nagle's algorithm)
    - Proper shutdown sequence (shutdown + close)
    - Statistics: tx_bytes, rx_bytes

UDP Transport (dvbs2x_udp.h)

  Connectionless unreliable transport for low-latency real-time
  streaming.  Suitable for satellite links where application-layer
  FEC (DVB-S2X LDPC) already provides error correction.

  Features:
    - Automatic retry on EINTR
    - MTU awareness (returns -EMSGSIZE if packet too large)
    - Statistics: tx_packets, rx_packets, tx_bytes, rx_bytes


API Usage
---------

Ring Buffer:

	/*
	 * Ring buffer capacity calculation:
	 *
	 * Required capacity (samples) =
	 *     sample_rate * oversampling * latency_budget
	 *
	 * Example for DVB-S2X IQ samples:
	 *   Symbol rate: 2 Msps
	 *   Oversampling: 4x (typical for RRC filter)
	 *   Latency budget: 100 ms (safe for context switches)
	 *
	 *   Capacity = 2e6 * 4 * 0.1 = 800,000 samples
	 *   Round up to power of 2: 1,048,576 (2^20)
	 *
	 * For lower rates or shorter latency:
	 *   1 Msps, 2x, 50ms -> 100,000 samples -> 131,072 (2^17)
	 *
	 * Note: 4096 samples shown below is for DEMO only!
	 * Production systems need much larger buffers.
	 */

	struct dvbs2x_ringbuf rb;
	struct dvbs2x_complex buffer[1048576];  /* 2^20, ~100ms @ 8 MSps */

	dvbs2x_ringbuf_init(&rb, buffer, 1048576,
			    sizeof(struct dvbs2x_complex));

	/* Producer writes */
	struct dvbs2x_complex samples[100];
	dvbs2x_ringbuf_put_batch(&rb, samples, 100);

	/* Consumer reads */
	struct dvbs2x_complex output[100];
	dvbs2x_ringbuf_get_batch(&rb, output, 100);

Protocol Layer:

	uint8_t frame_buf[2048];
	uint8_t payload[1024];
	uint8_t output[1024];
	enum dvbs2x_frame_type frame_type;
	uint32_t sequence;
	uint16_t stream_id;
	int ret;

	/* Encode frame */
	ret = dvbs2x_frame_encode(frame_buf, sizeof(frame_buf),
				   payload, 1024,
				   DVBS2X_FRAME_IQ_SAMPLES,
				   seq_num++, 0);
	if (ret < 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Decode frame */
	ret = dvbs2x_frame_decode(frame_buf, ret, output,
				   sizeof(output), &frame_type,
				   &sequence, &stream_id);
	if (ret < 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Sequence number comparison with wrap handling */
	uint32_t expected_seq = 0xFFFFFFFE;  /* Near wrap point */
	uint32_t received_seq = 0x00000001;  /* After wrap */

	if (dvbs2x_seq_after(received_seq, expected_seq)) {
		/* received_seq is after expected_seq (correct!) */
		printf("Sequence advanced correctly\n");
	}

	/* Check if sequence is in order */
	static uint32_t last_seq = 0;
	if (dvbs2x_seq_after(sequence, last_seq)) {
		last_seq = sequence;  /* Update */
	} else {
		printf("Out-of-order frame: expected > %u, got %u\n",
		       last_seq, sequence);
	}

TCP Server:

	struct dvbs2x_tcp_ctx server, client;
	uint8_t buffer[2048];
	int ret;

	dvbs2x_platform_init();

	/* Bind to port */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_server_init(&server, 5000);
	if (ret != 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Accept connection (1 second timeout) */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_server_accept(&server, &client, 1000);
	if (ret != 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Receive data (all bytes or timeout) */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_recv(&client, buffer, 2048, 5000);
	if (ret < 0)
		handle_error(-ret);
	else if (ret == 0)
		printf("Connection closed\n");
	else
		printf("Received %d bytes\n", ret);

	/* Send data (blocks until all sent) */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_send(&client, buffer, ret);
	if (ret < 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	dvbs2x_tcp_close(&client);
	dvbs2x_tcp_close(&server);
	dvbs2x_platform_cleanup();

TCP Client:

	struct dvbs2x_tcp_ctx client;
	uint8_t buffer[2048];
	int ret;

	dvbs2x_platform_init();

	/* Connect to server (3 second timeout) */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_client_connect(&client, "192.168.1.10",
					5000, 3000);
	if (ret != 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Send data */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_send(&client, buffer, 2048);
	if (ret < 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Receive response */
	ret = dvbs2x_tcp_recv(&client, buffer, 2048, 5000);
	if (ret > 0)
		printf("Received %d bytes\n", ret);

	dvbs2x_tcp_close(&client);
	dvbs2x_platform_cleanup();

UDP:

	struct dvbs2x_udp_ctx udp;
	uint8_t buffer[1400];  /* Keep under MTU! */
	int ret;

	dvbs2x_platform_init();

	/* Bind to local port */
	ret = dvbs2x_udp_init(&udp, 5001);
	if (ret != 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Set peer address */
	ret = dvbs2x_udp_set_peer(&udp, "192.168.1.10", 5002);
	if (ret != 0)
		handle_error(-ret);

	/* Send datagram */
	ret = dvbs2x_udp_send(&udp, buffer, 1400);
	if (ret < 0) {
		if (ret == -EMSGSIZE)
			printf("Packet too large for MTU\n");
		else
			handle_error(-ret);
	}

	/* Receive datagram (2 second timeout) */
	ret = dvbs2x_udp_recv(&udp, buffer, sizeof(buffer), 2000);
	if (ret > 0)
		printf("Received %d bytes\n", ret);
	else if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT)
		printf("Timeout\n");

	dvbs2x_udp_close(&udp);
	dvbs2x_platform_cleanup();


Design Notes
------------

  - Lock-free ring buffer uses atomic memory ordering (ATOMIC_ACQUIRE/
    ATOMIC_RELEASE) to ensure correctness without locks.  Suitable for
    real-time systems where mutex overhead is unacceptable.

  - Platform abstractions are compile-time selected via preprocessor
    macros.  Inline functions eliminate function call overhead for
    socket operations.  Zero runtime cost.

  - Ring buffer head and tail are on separate cache lines (64 bytes)
    to prevent false sharing between producer and consumer threads.

  - Thread priority and CPU affinity support enables real-time
    scheduling for satellite communication (SCHED_FIFO on Linux).

  - Memory locking (mlock) prevents page faults in real-time paths.

  - TCP send/recv loop until all bytes are transferred.  Critical
    for real networks where partial sends/receives are common.

  - TCP_NODELAY disables Nagle's algorithm for low latency (40-200ms
    reduction on small packets).

  - All system calls retry on EINTR (signal interruption).

  - Proper TCP shutdown sequence (shutdown before close) ensures
    buffered data is flushed.


Performance
-----------

Measured on Intel x86_64 @ 3.0 GHz (Linux):

  CRC-32:              550-766 MB/s
  Protocol encode:     ~460 MB/s
  Protocol decode:     ~420 MB/s
  Ring buffer batch:   17 GB/s (128-byte batches)
  Frame overhead:      <1 µs per frame (typical)


Limitations
-----------

Ring Buffer:
  - Single-producer single-consumer only.  Multiple producers or
    multiple consumers require external locking.
  - Power-of-2 buffer sizes are enforced (checked at init time).

Network:
  - IPv4 only (AF_INET hardcoded).  IPv6 support requires changes.
  - UDP maximum practical payload: ~1400 bytes (MTU limit).
    Larger payloads will cause IP fragmentation or EMSGSIZE error.
    Use protocol-level fragmentation for large frames over UDP.
  - TCP/UDP contexts are not thread-safe.  Each context should be
    used by a single thread.  Statistics counters are not atomic.

Platform:
  - Real-time thread priorities require CAP_SYS_NICE capability or
    root privileges on Linux.
  - Windows support is compile-time enabled but not fully tested.


Important Notes for Production Deployment
------------------------------------------

UDP MTU Considerations:
  - Localhost MTU is typically 65KB (no fragmentation visible).
  - Real Ethernet MTU is 1500 bytes.
  - UDP payload should not exceed ~1400 bytes to avoid fragmentation.
  - IP fragmentation causes poor performance and packet loss.
  - Many routers and firewalls drop fragmented packets.
  - For satellite links, MTU may be even smaller (1200-1400 bytes).

Recommendation:
  - Limit UDP payload to 1400 bytes maximum.
  - Use protocol-level fragmentation (MORE_FRAGS flag) for larger
    frames, with reassembly at receiver.
  - Or use TCP for large frames (automatic segmentation/reassembly).

Network Testing:
  - Localhost testing does NOT reveal network issues.
  - Test on real networks (different subnets, WAN links).
  - Use tc/netem to simulate latency and packet loss:
      tc qdisc add dev eth0 root netem delay 100ms loss 1%
  - Test with background traffic (iperf) to simulate congestion.
  - Test across NAT/firewall boundaries.

Signal Handling:
  - All blocking calls retry on EINTR (signal interruption).
  - If using signals in your application, this is handled correctly.

Real-Time Considerations:
  - Set thread priority and CPU affinity for deterministic latency.
  - Lock memory (mlock) to prevent page faults.
  - Disable frequency scaling (use performance governor).
  - Isolate CPU cores (isolcpus kernel parameter).


